                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Exhibiting homoiconicity."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "not-comparable"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "22dbe596-e007-41de-b34f-ead63a5b3ce6",
                                                "sense_id": "ba05c321-17b9-319a-8f47-8065bb7cd4e0",
                                                "text": "Instead, the homoiconic paradigm draws from the mathematics of computability theory. […] In the case of a homoiconic programming language, the program processes itself, performing mechanical reductions on the application until there is nothing left except the answer."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "ed84d5d1-8e9a-46b0-9c93-a1c5683cfd44",
                                                "sense_id": "ba05c321-17b9-319a-8f47-8065bb7cd4e0",
                                                "text": "Clojure and other languages from the Lisp family are homoiconic. In a homoiconic language, the source code of a program is represented as a plain data structure."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
